{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>וַיִיָחֶל</b> — from the nif'al binyan, coming as a transitive (kil'el) like וַיִלָּחֶם (Numbers 21:1), וַיִצָמֶד (Numbers 25:3). And there are [forms] from this binyan that are milra, like וַיִפָּקֵד מְקוֹם דָּוִד (1 Samuel 20:27). And the yod comes because the root is complete with yod, like 'או ירה יִיָרֶה' (Exodus 19:13)." "And on the first of Adar he sent out the dove, and it did not again, as was its habit, return to him," "'still': forever. Likewise עוֹד טֻמְאָתוֹ בוֹ (Numbers 19:13), עוֹד כָּל יְמֵי הָאָרֶץ (Genesis 8:22)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "The final comparison invokes roots whose first radical is yod; Ibn Ezra's phrasing 'בשרשו השלם' means the root retains all three radical letters without loss."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:43:48.880213299Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}